  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 1224 AM MDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nA significant and potentially dangerous period of monsoon moisture \nand two merging storm systems over the Desert Southwest is favored \nto begin Monday. PWATs spike to 1.20\", potentially higher 1.30+\" by \nTuesday. With a stout speedmax rotating around the southeastern \nportion of the 569dm H5 low, storm speeds across the area will be \nfast, with a few storms being capable of organizing and threatening \nsome brief severe weather. Storms training over the same areas \nrepeatedly will again threaten flash flooding Monday. \n\nDeterministic runs of the GFS/ECMWF/Canadian have come into very \ngood agreement that the remnants of Hurricane Polo will absorb \ninto the H5 low ahead of the system on its southeastern periphery \nTuesday. This particularly dynamic will act to slow down the \nprogress of the system writ large across AZ/NM. A period of \nwidespread showers and thunderstorms across most of the state \nMonday night through Tuesday and Wednesday will be favored with \nthis. Multi-day precipitation amounts would favor ranges of 1 to \n4 inches across much of New Mexico Monday thru Wednesday. This \nwill exacerbate current ongoing flooding recovery efforts next \nweek. Further rises on area tributaries, streams, and rivers is \nexpected.\n\nThe H5 low continues across northern NM Thursday with precipitation \nfavoring northeastern NM the longest thru Thursday evening before \nentirely clearing out of the area. Thereafter, more tranquil weather \nconditions are favored Friday and into next weekend.",
